select 'culture-socialization', 'Strain theory (sociology): Institutional Anomie theory', 'Institutional anomie theory (IAT) is a macro-level criminological theory developed in 1994 by Steven Messner and Richard Rosenfeld. Messner and Rosenfeld agree with Merton''s view that American culture overemphasizes financial success, and expand on his view by arguing that the economy''s supremacy over other social institutions and the cultural values of the American Dream create high crime rates. Messner and Rosenfeld argue that in American culture economic success is valued above everything else, which socializes Americans to value: individualism: focus on monetary success rather than who you are as a person and gaining success through your own hard work, ''no free handouts'' universalism: the idea that all members of a society are socialized to value monetary success, and all members have equal opportunity to attain success as long as they work hard the ''fetishism of money''" (Messner and Rosenfeld 2001:68): a specific strain of materialism, US obsession with wealth as the primary marker of success or worth', 'Wikipedia: Strain theory (sociology)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Strain_theory_(sociology)&oldid=1380194430', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'culture-socialization' and body = 'Institutional anomie theory (IAT) is a macro-level criminological theory developed in 1994 by Steven Messner and Richard Rosenfeld. Messner and Rosenfeld agree with Merton''s view that American culture overemphasizes financial success, and expand on his view by arguing that the economy''s supremacy over other social institutions and the cultural values of the American Dream create high crime rates. Messner and Rosenfeld argue that in American culture economic success is valued above everything else, which socializes Americans to value: individualism: focus on monetary success rather than who you are as a person and gaining success through your own hard work, ''no free handouts'' universalism: the idea that all members of a society are socialized to value monetary success, and all members have equal opportunity to attain success as long as they work hard the ''fetishism of money''" (Messner and Rosenfeld 2001:68): a specific strain of materialism, US obsession with wealth as the primary marker of success or worth');
